  ],
  "text": "A rocket works better in a vacuum. It is not pushing against anything. Everyone assumes it shoves against the air, the way a swimmer shoves against water. If that were true, a rocket would stop working the moment it left the atmosphere. What it actually does is throw mass backwards, extremely fast. The rocket pushes the exhaust one way and the exhaust pushes the rocket the other. That is the whole mechanism. Two things shoving each other apart. Stand on a skateboard, throw something heavy, and you roll backwards. Same thing exactly. Air does not help at all. Air is drag, and it squeezes the exhaust plume inwards too. Which is why upper stage engines have enormous nozzles. With no air pressing in, the plume spreads. A rocket needs nothing to push against. It brings the thing it pushes against with it. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
